And to ensure a random glitch doesn't destroy the treaty,

21:21.920 --> 21:25.700
they employ quantum error correction, or QEC.

21:26.180 --> 21:30.300
Okay, we hear about QEC in the context of building quantum computers,

21:30.740 --> 21:33.000
but how does it apply to thermodynamic trust?

21:33.320 --> 21:37.320
Well, the fundamental problem in quantum mechanics is that if you look at a quantum state,

21:37.600 --> 21:39.340
if you measure it directly, it collapses.

21:39.800 --> 21:41.240
It loses its superposition.

21:41.240 --> 21:41.520
Right.

21:42.020 --> 21:42.820
Schrodinger's cat.

21:42.820 --> 21:43.380
Exactly.

21:43.740 --> 21:47.460
So how do you know if an error occurred without looking at the data and destroying it?

21:48.040 --> 21:52.100
QEC uses something called surface codes mapped onto a 2D grid.

21:52.600 --> 21:56.340
Instead of storing one piece of logical information in one physical quibbit,

21:56.620 --> 22:01.040
the information is smeared across dozens of quibbits in a topological lattice.

22:01.140 --> 22:02.160
So it's highly redundant.

22:02.420 --> 22:06.320
It is redundant, but the brilliance is in how it checks for errors.

22:06.800 --> 22:10.920
The system uses Ancilla quibbits, helper quibbits, interspersed in the grid.

22:10.920 --> 22:12.760
It doesn't measure the data itself.

22:12.940 --> 22:15.300
It measures the parity between neighboring quibbits.

22:15.560 --> 22:17.600
It checks the geometry of the relationship.

22:17.800 --> 22:18.320
Oh, that's clever.

22:18.320 --> 22:24.720
If a cosmic ray flips a single physical quibbit, the surface code detects the anomaly, the syndrome,

22:24.960 --> 22:31.180
